//
// Implemented by nervud as a **built-in endpoint**, read-only.
//
// # The real gap it fills
//
// A ResourceSelector can answer "connect me to that front camera", but not
// "how many cameras does this machine actually have". Without the latter, Apps could only probe with repeated Resolves
// until NOT_FOUND. That's slow and unreliable: a failed Resolve can't tell
// "no such device" from "there is one but I'm not allowed".
//
// With a directory, device discovery becomes one query, and Resolve goes back to its real job: setting up routes.
//
// # Why the kernel implements it rather than a system service
//
// The resource directory **is the Catalog itself**. Having a service answer for it would mean it maintaining a copy of the Catalog,
// and between a copy and the original there's always a window that can drift: an install has taken effect, the copy hasn't
// refreshed yet, and the App gets a stale device list. The kernel reads its own Snapshot directly,
// so that window doesn't exist.
//
// # Boundary: it **only** holds information a successful Resolve would already expose
//
// What's returned is the public projection: handle / type / role / access mode / risk level / labels.
// It does **not** include device node paths, serial numbers, the Provider PID or vendor firmware versions.
//
// This boundary is hard: the directory is readable by every App holding perm.resource.query, so
// adding a field "just returned as well" would publish that field to the whole system.
// Callers needing finer information should Resolve to the specific interface and accept that interface's own permission decision there.
//
